OpenLiveGamer4K DKMS package
============================

This is an beta test package. It installs the source-only V4L2
capture driver for the AVerMedia Live Gamer 4K (GC573, PCI ID 1461:0054)
and registers it with DKMS, which builds and installs the kernel module
`gc573_pure` for installed kernels that have matching headers.

Intended targets: Ubuntu 24.04 (Noble) and Linux Mint 22.x on amd64, with
the target kernel's linux-headers package installed. Tested on Linux Mint
22.3, kernel 6.17.0-22-generic; Ubuntu 24.04 is intended but untested.
Other distributions, architectures and kernel tracks are unqualified.

What this package does
----------------------
- Installs DKMS build inputs under /usr/src/gc573_pure-0.1.0~beta2/.
- Registers the source with DKMS and builds/installs the module for
  eligible installed kernels (maintainer scripts generated by dh_dkms).
- Installs documentation in /usr/share/doc/openlivegamer4k-dkms/.

What this package does NOT do
-----------------------------
- It does not load or unload a module, unbind or rebind a PCI device,
  or touch a running capture session.
- It does not create /etc/modules-load.d entries, modprobe option
  files, or driver blacklists.
- It does not sign modules, enroll a MOK, or change Secure Boot
  policy. With Secure Boot enabled, DKMS signing uses the distro's
  standard MOK workflow; a build success does not mean the module can
  load until the key is enrolled (see TROUBLESHOOTING).
- Installing the package is NOT capture-ready. See below.

Capture activation is not automatic
-----------------------------------
The PCI device table gives the module a normal modalias, so the kernel
may load gc573_pure automatically when the card is probed. Parameters
passed to `modprobe` apply only when a module is first inserted; they
cannot reconfigure an already-loaded module. Capture requires these
options on the FIRST load of the boot:

    bridge_link=1 bridge_bootstrap=1 reset_gpio_mask=288 \
    preserve_bridge=0 preserve_receiver=0 test_hpd_after_ms=0

First use, after installing this package:

1. Make sure no capture application (for example OBS) is using the card.
2. Check whether the module is already loaded:
       lsmod | grep gc573_pure
   If it is loaded by modalias handling without the options above, it
   cannot be reconfigured without unloading it. Only do that when no
   capture client uses the device:
       sudo modprobe -r gc573_pure
3. Load with the required options:
       sudo modprobe gc573_pure bridge_link=1 bridge_bootstrap=1 \
           reset_gpio_mask=288 preserve_bridge=0 preserve_receiver=0 \
           test_hpd_after_ms=0
4. Read back the live parameters and verify each value:
       for p in bridge_link bridge_bootstrap reset_gpio_mask \
                preserve_bridge preserve_receiver test_hpd_after_ms; do
           printf '%s=' "$p"; cat /sys/module/gc573_pure/parameters/$p; done
5. Verify the video node exists (usually /dev/video0):
       v4l2-ctl --list-devices
       ls -l /dev/video* /dev/v4l/by-path/ 2>/dev/null

Next-boot behavior
------------------
This package ships no persistent option profile. After a reboot the
module may autoload again without the bridge options. If that happens,
repeat steps 2-5 after stopping any capture client. Do not treat the
one-shot command above as a next-boot policy.

Removing or rolling back
------------------------
Stop capture applications and unload the module first:

    sudo modprobe -r gc573_pure
    sudo apt remove openlivegamer4k-dkms        # keeps nothing (no conffiles)
    sudo apt purge openlivegamer4k-dkms         # also removes docs

Removal unregisters the DKMS source and removes built modules through
the standard DKMS lifecycle. It never unloads a module in use and does
not restore any previously bound driver binding; use the recorded PCI
BDF and that driver's documented procedure to restore it.

